<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hryhorii Skovoroda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Grigory Skovoroda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hryhorii Skovoroda (1722–1794) was a Ukrainian philosopher, poet and teacher, educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a>. After teaching at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv-collegium">Kharkiv Collegium</a> he spent his last decades as a wandering thinker in Sloboda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, writing philosophical dialogues, fables and the verse collection Garden of Divine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> on self-knowledge and work suited to one's nature. He is regarded as a founding figure of Ukrainian philosophy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hryhorii-skovoroda.jpg" alt="Hryhorii Skovoroda" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: portrait by unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hryhoriy_Skovoroda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hryhorii Skovoroda was born on 3 December 1722 into a small-holder Registered Cossack family in the village of Chornukhy. His mother was partly of Crimean Tatar descent. He studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a> in several periods between 1734 and 1753 but did not graduate. In 1741 he was taken to sing in the imperial choir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, and from 1745 to 1750 he was in the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> with a commission buying Tokaj wine for the imperial court, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and meeting local scholars. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in 1750.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He taught poetics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> collegium in 1750 and 1751, until a dispute over his methods led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> to dismiss him. From 1753 to 1759 he tutored the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> noble Stepan Tomara and began composing poems for The Garden of Divine Songs. From 1759 to 1769, with interruptions, he taught poetry, syntax, Greek and ethics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv-collegium">Kharkiv Collegium</a>. He refused pressure to take monastic vows. His closest student there was Mikhail Kovalensky, who became his first biographer. After a bishop objected to an unordained teacher giving catechism, he resigned in April 1769 and never held an institutional post again.</p><p>He then lived as a wandering philosopher, first at an apiary near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a>, where he wrote philosophical fables and dialogues such as Narcissus and the Symphony Called the Book of Askhan. He travelled on foot through Sloboda Ukraine, staying with friends rich and poor and forming a circle of admirers. He also composed liturgical music and songs, and several of his philosophical songs entered the repertoire of kobzar folk musicians. In his last year he walked to visit Kovalensky near Oryol and entrusted him with his collected works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Skovoroda's works were not printed in his lifetime because of church censorship. His first book appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1798, and the first almost complete edition came in 1861. His native speech was vernacular Ukrainian, but he wrote in a mixture of Church Slavonic, Ukrainian and Russian, and most of his surviving letters are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek. His treatises and dialogues join biblical questions to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and the Stoics.</p><p>He taught that the kingdom of God is within a person, that to know God one must know oneself, and that all work is blessed by God. Each person should seek a congenial work, since those who abandon their calling to chase wealth or prestige become unhappy. He died on 9 November 1794 in Pan-Ivanovka.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hryhorii Skovoroda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The philosopher <b>Hryhorii Skovoroda</b> taught at the Kharkiv Collegium.</blockquote><a href="/state/sloboda-ukraine">Sloboda Ukraine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chornukhy, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Skovorodynivka, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.27,32.95&amp;year=1768" aria-label="Chornukhy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hryhorii Skovoroda"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5161 5825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5161 5825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.27,32.95&amp;year=1768">What was here in 1768? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
